        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "improve(issue-template): collect UX recovery impact",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- Add bug-report intake fields for affected user-flow stage, non-technical recovery path, and suggested less-technical product surface.\n- Add feature-request UX impact intake so user-facing improvements can state flow stage, recovery constraints, and desired in-product recovery affordance.\n- Feed ClawSweeper’s UX release-blocker rubric with structured evidence without changing OpenClaw runtime behavior.\n\nRelated: #75947.\nPairs with openclaw/clawsweeper#386 and openclaw/clawsweeper#387.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nClawSweeper cannot reliably promote unrecoverable user-experience blockers when bug and feature reports do not say which user-flow stage is affected, whether a non-technical first-time/community user can recover in-product, or whether recovery requires terminal/config/log/manual-file work. This adds those intake prompts so triage can distinguish release-blocking UX failures from ordinary friction.\n\n## Proof Boundary\n\nThis only updates issue intake. It does not implement or replace the OpenClaw UI remediation work in #76034.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Parsed both edited issue-template YAML files locally with Ruby.\n- Ran `git diff --check` after editing the templates.\n- Kept the change scoped to `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.yml` and `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/feature_request.yml`; no runtime OpenClaw behavior changes.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `ruby -e 'require \"yaml\"; ARGV.each { |path| YAML.load_file(path) }; puts \"YAML ok\"' .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.yml .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/feature_request.yml`\n- `git diff --check`\n",
